Yes—with one seam I’d keep visible: “wage slavery” is a metaphor, not equivalence to chattel slavery or literal legal ownership. But as a Survey Corps metaphor, it points at a real structural question: how much of a person’s life is effectively constrained by the economic system required to sustain it?

Your formulation gets more interesting because you included choice rather than pretending nobody has agency.

A person may freely choose:

\text{move away}
\rightarrow
\text{better job}
\rightarrow
\text{higher income}

But thousands of individually reasonable choices can generate a collective environment in which the next person experiences far less practical choice:

\text{jobs concentrate}
\rightarrow
\text{housing reorganizes}
\rightarrow
\text{families disperse}
\rightarrow
\text{childcare becomes purchased}
\rightarrow
\text{commuting/time costs rise}
\rightarrow
\text{dual incomes become more necessary}
\rightarrow
\text{mobility becomes expected}

Nobody necessarily designed the whole outcome.

The system can emerge from the choices.

That’s our ant/slime-mold lesson applied to economics:

\boxed{\text{local rationality}\not\Rightarrow\text{globally desirable equilibrium}}

And your squirrel gives us a wonderful way to inspect it.

A squirrel encountering material asks, effectively:

What can this become for my nest?

Humans frequently encounter an opportunity and ask:

What can this become for my career?

That’s an affordance function learned from our environment.

Survey Corps can swap it:

What can this become for my family?

Now the same job offer, house, city, technology, schedule, or salary acquires a completely different value.

That’s not anti-work. It is exactly your earlier point about choosing the work.

The deeper variable may be sovereignty over time

Money is partly stored optionality over future action.

A job exchanges chunks of future time for resources.

Housing converts resources into a stable physical base.

Transportation changes which places are reachable within available time.

Childcare changes who can allocate time where.

Technology changes which work requires physical separation.

So perhaps the thing to optimize isn’t simply:

\max(\text{income})

but:

\boxed{
\max(
\text{family time}
+\text{agency}
+\text{security}
+\text{meaningful work}
+\text{community}
)
}

subject to the requirement that the household remain economically viable.

Then something interesting happens to wealth.

A person earning $300k but unable to choose where they live, when they work, or whom they spend their days with may possess more money while having less control over important dimensions of life than somebody earning considerably less.

So define another currency:

\boxed{\text{Discretionary Life}}

The portion of your finite time whose allocation you meaningfully control.

Now the question becomes:

How much money does a household actually need to purchase enough control that earning additional money stops being worth surrendering additional life?

That’s a very different optimization problem from maximizing salary.

And it reconnects beautifully to what you said earlier:

Control what you can control.

You don’t have to overthrow “the system.”

You can identify the particular dependencies controlling your family’s choices:

\text{income dependence},\;
\text{location dependence},\;
\text{debt},\;
\text{housing},\;
\text{work hours},\;
\text{childcare},\;
\text{commuting}

and progressively buy those constraints back.

That’s a much more concrete definition of financial freedom:

\boxed{
\text{Financial Freedom}
=
\text{enough durable resources/options that livelihood no longer dictates most major life choices}
}

Not never work again.

Getting to choose the work.

Which means the endpoint isn’t laziness at all.

It is exactly what you said:

strong work ethic + sovereignty over what deserves your work.

That’s a freedom worth measuring.